-- url     : https://prove2.me/theorems/6bfa63f6-da31-4160-9443-6fec99c5ded5
-- title:
--   Sections 2–3 — the Itô market (2.3)–(2.10), the Itô integral operator, the wealth equation (3.1) and the class $\mathcal A_0(x)$
-- statement:
--   Let $(\Omega,\mathcal F,P)$ be a complete probability space carrying a standard Brownian motion $W=(W_1,\dots,W_d)^*$ in $\mathbb R^d$, and let $\{\mathcal F_t\}$ be the $P$-augmentation of the filtration $\mathcal F^W_t=\sigma(W(s);\,0\le s\le t)$: for every $t$, $\mathcal F_t$ is generated by the random variables $W(s)$, $s\le t$, together with the $P$-null sets. Fix a horizon $T>0$. Throughout, $\ell$ is Lebesgue measure and $\ell\otimes P$ the product measure on $[0,T]\times\Omega$.
--
--   **Stochastic integrals.** A process $\varphi$ with values in $\mathbb R^d$ is *locally square integrable* if it is $\{\mathcal F_t\}$-progressively measurable and $\int_0^T\|\varphi(t)\|^2\,dt<\infty$ almost surely. A real process $J$ is the Itô integral $\int_0^\cdot\varphi^*(s)\,dW(s)=\sum_j\int_0^\cdot\varphi_j\,dW_j$ on $[0,T]$ if $J$ is adapted with almost surely continuous paths on $[0,T]$ and, for each $t\le T$, there are bounded elementary predictable integrands on the dyadic partitions of $[0,t]$ whose integrated squared distance to each $\varphi_j$ tends to $0$ in probability and whose Itô sums tend to $J(t)$ in probability. An *Itô-integral operator* is a map $I$ that assigns to every locally square-integrable $\varphi$ such a process $I\varphi$.
--
--   **The market** $\mathcal M$ (pp. 769–770) has a bond and $d$ stocks with progressively measurable coefficients: the interest rate $r(t)$, the vector of appreciation rates $b(t)$ and the volatility matrix $\sigma(t)$. The standing assumptions are, for real constants $\eta\ge0$, $\varepsilon>0$,
--   $$r(t)\ge-\eta\quad\forall\,0\le t\le T,\qquad \xi^*\sigma(t)\sigma^*(t)\xi\ge\varepsilon\|\xi\|^2\quad\forall\,(t,\xi)\in[0,T]\times\mathbb R^d \tag{2.3–2.4}$$
--   almost surely, $E\int_0^T r(s)\,ds<\infty$ (2.5), and the finite-energy condition $E\int_0^T\|\theta(t)\|^2\,dt<\infty$ (2.7) for the relative risk process
--   $$\theta(t)=\sigma^{-1}(t)\,[\,b(t)-r(t)\mathbf 1\,],\qquad \mathbf 1=(1,\dots,1)^*. \tag{2.6}$$
--   With these, the exponential local martingale, the discount process and the state-price density are
--   $$Z_0(t)=\exp\Big[-\int_0^t\theta^*(s)\,dW(s)-\tfrac12\int_0^t\|\theta(s)\|^2ds\Big],\quad \gamma_0(t)=\exp\Big\{-\int_0^t r(s)\,ds\Big\},\quad H_0(t)=\gamma_0(t)Z_0(t). \tag{2.8–2.10}$$
--
--   **Portfolios, consumption, wealth** (Definition 3.1). A *portfolio process* $\pi$ is an $\mathbb R^d$-valued, progressively measurable process with $\int_0^T\|\pi(t)\|^2dt<\infty$ a.s.; a *consumption process* $c$ is a nonnegative progressively measurable process with $\int_0^Tc(t)\,dt<\infty$ a.s. The *wealth process* $X=X^{x,\pi,c}$ of the pair $(\pi,c)$ with initial capital $x\in(0,\infty)$ is the solution of
--   $$dX(t)=[\,r(t)X(t)-c(t)\,]\,dt+X(t)\pi^*(t)\sigma(t)\,dW_0(t),\qquad X(0)=x,\qquad W_0(t)=W(t)+\int_0^t\theta(s)\,ds. \tag{3.1–3.2}$$
--   A pair $(\pi,c)$ is *admissible* for $x$, written $(\pi,c)\in\mathcal A_0(x)$, if $X^{x,\pi,c}(t)\ge0$ for all $0\le t\le T$, almost surely (Definition 3.2, (3.3)).
--
--   These objects are the substrate of every statement of the mission.
--
--   **Formalization Note** Processes are functions $\mathbb R_{\ge0}\times\Omega\to E$; only times $t\le T$ are constrained. Time integrals are Lebesgue integrals over $[0,t]\subset\mathbb R$. The stochastic integral is an explicit binder $I$, constrained only by the hypothesis that it is an Itô-integral operator (the classical construction provides one, so the hypothesis is satisfiable). (3.1) is stated in integral form after substituting $dW_0=dW+\theta\,dt$: $X(t)=x+\int_0^t(rX-c+X\,\pi^*\sigma\theta)\,ds+\int_0^tX(\sigma^*\pi)^*dW$, with the requirement that both integrals exist; $X$ is adapted, has a.s. continuous paths on $[0,T]$, and the equation holds a.s. for every $t\le T$. Because the linear equation has a unique solution up to indistinguishability, the classes are sets of triples $(\pi,c,X)$. The expectation in (2.5) is that of $r^+$ (equivalent, since $r\ge-\eta$). $\sigma^{-1}$ is the matrix inverse, invertible a.s. by (2.4). The Brownian motion is the published `EthierKurtz.IsStandardBrownian`; the Itô integral is an almost-sure variant of `EthierKurtz.HasBrownianItoIntegral`, which requires square integrability for every $\omega$, while Definition 3.1 asks it only almost surely.
-- source:
--   Cvitanić and Karatzas, Convex Duality in Constrained Portfolio Optimization, Ann. Appl. Probab. 2 (1992), pp. 769–771, Sections 2–3, (2.1)–(2.10), Definitions 3.1–3.2, (3.1)–(3.3)

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_EthierKurtz_IsStandardBrownian
import Definitions.Def_EthierKurtz_itoStepValue
import Definitions.Def_EthierKurtz_itoStepSum

open MeasureTheory ProbabilityTheory Filter
open scoped ENNReal NNReal Topology BigOperators

namespace CvitanicKaratzas92.Optimality

variable {Ω : Type*} [mΩ : MeasurableSpace Ω] {d : ℕ}

/-- Cvitanić–Karatzas (1992), p. 769: `𝓕` is the `P`-augmentation of the filtration
`𝓕_t^W = σ(W(s); 0 ≤ s ≤ t)` generated by `W`: at every time it is generated by the
past of `W` together with the `P`-null sets. -/
def IsAugmentedBrownianFiltration (P : Measure Ω) (W : ℝ≥0 → Ω → EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin d))
    (𝓕 : Filtration ℝ≥0 mΩ) : Prop :=
  ∀ t : ℝ≥0, (𝓕 t : MeasurableSpace Ω) =
    (⨆ s : ℝ≥0, ⨆ (_ : s ≤ t), MeasurableSpace.comap (W s) inferInstance) ⊔
      MeasurableSpace.generateFrom {N | P N = 0}

/-- The product measure `ℓ ⊗ P` on `[0, T] × Ω` (`ℓ` = Lebesgue measure), used for every
"`ℓ ⊗ P`-a.e. `(t, ω)`" statement of the paper. A pair `q` stands for `(q.1.toNNReal, q.2)`. -/
noncomputable def lebP (P : Measure Ω) (T : ℝ≥0) : Measure (ℝ × Ω) :=
  (volume.restrict (Set.Icc (0 : ℝ) T)).prod P

/-- Definition 3.1 (i), p. 770: an `ℝᵈ`-valued, `{𝓕_t}`-progressively measurable process with
`∫₀ᵀ ‖φ(t)‖² dt < ∞` almost surely. -/
def IsLocallySquareIntegrable (𝓕 : Filtration ℝ≥0 mΩ) (P : Measure Ω) (T : ℝ≥0)
    (φ : ℝ≥0 → Ω → EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin d)) : Prop :=
  IsStronglyProgressive 𝓕 φ ∧
    ∀ᵐ ω ∂P, IntegrableOn (fun s : ℝ => ‖φ s.toNNReal ω‖ ^ 2) (Set.Icc (0 : ℝ) T)

/-- `J = ∑ⱼ ∫ φⱼ dWⱼ` on `[0, T]`: `φ` is locally square integrable, `J` is adapted with
almost surely continuous paths on `[0, T]`, and for every `t ≤ T` there are bounded elementary
predictable integrands (coefficients measurable at the left endpoint of their dyadic interval)
that approximate each coordinate `φⱼ` in integrated squared error in probability and whose
finite Itô sums converge in probability to `J t`. This is the almost-sure variant of
`EthierKurtz.HasBrownianItoIntegral`. -/
def IsItoIntegral (P : Measure Ω) (𝓕 : Filtration ℝ≥0 mΩ) (T : ℝ≥0)
    (W : ℝ≥0 → Ω → EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin d)) (φ : ℝ≥0 → Ω → EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin d))
    (J : ℝ≥0 → Ω → ℝ) : Prop :=
  IsLocallySquareIntegrable 𝓕 P T φ ∧
  (∀ t, Measurable[𝓕 t] (J t)) ∧
  (∀ᵐ ω ∂P, ContinuousOn (fun t => J t ω) (Set.Iic T)) ∧
  ∀ t ≤ T, ∃ a : Fin d → ℕ → ℕ → Ω → ℝ,
    (∀ (j : Fin d) (n k : ℕ), k < 2 ^ n → Measurable[𝓕 ((k : ℝ≥0) * t / (2 : ℝ≥0) ^ n)] (a j n k)) ∧
    (∀ j n, ∃ C : ℝ, ∀ k, k < 2 ^ n → ∀ ω, |a j n k ω| ≤ C) ∧
    (∀ j, TendstoInMeasure P
      (fun n ω => ∫ u in (0 : ℝ)..t.val,
        (EthierKurtz.itoStepValue t n (a j n) u ω - φ u.toNNReal ω j) ^ 2)
      atTop (fun _ => (0 : ℝ))) ∧
    TendstoInMeasure P
      (fun n ω => ∑ j, EthierKurtz.itoStepSum (fun s ω => W s ω j) t n (a j n) ω)
      atTop (J t)

/-- `I` is an Itô-integral operator: for every locally square-integrable integrand `φ`,
`I φ` is a version of `∫ φ* dW` on `[0, T]`. This pins `I φ` down up to indistinguishability on
the integrands that matter, and is satisfiable by the classical construction of the Itô
integral. -/
def IsItoIntegralOperator (P : Measure Ω) (𝓕 : Filtration ℝ≥0 mΩ) (T : ℝ≥0)
    (W : ℝ≥0 → Ω → EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin d))
    (I : (ℝ≥0 → Ω → EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin d)) → ℝ≥0 → Ω → ℝ) : Prop :=
  ∀ φ, IsLocallySquareIntegrable 𝓕 P T φ → IsItoIntegral P 𝓕 T W φ (I φ)

/-- The financial market `𝓜` of Section 2 (p. 769): interest rate `r`, appreciation-rate vector
`b` and volatility matrix `σ`. -/
structure Market (Ω : Type*) (d : ℕ) where
  r : ℝ≥0 → Ω → ℝ
  b : ℝ≥0 → Ω → EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin d)
  σ : ℝ≥0 → Ω → Matrix (Fin d) (Fin d) ℝ

/-- The all-ones vector `1 = (1, …, 1)*`. -/
noncomputable def onesVec (d : ℕ) : EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin d) := WithLp.toLp 2 (fun _ => (1 : ℝ))

/-- (2.6): the relative-risk process `θ(t) = σ⁻¹(t)[b(t) − r(t)1]`. -/
noncomputable def theta (M : Market Ω d) (t : ℝ≥0) (ω : Ω) : EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin d) :=
  Matrix.toEuclideanLin (M.σ t ω)⁻¹ (M.b t ω - M.r t ω • onesVec d)

/-- Standing assumptions on the market (pp. 769): `r, b, σ` progressively measurable; (2.3)
`r(t) ≥ −η` for all `t ≤ T` a.s.; (2.4) `ξ*σ(t)σ*(t)ξ ≥ ε‖ξ‖²` for all `(t, ξ)` a.s.;
(2.5) `E∫₀ᵀ r(s) ds < ∞`; (2.7) `E∫₀ᵀ ‖θ(t)‖² dt < ∞`. -/
def Market.Standing (P : Measure Ω) (𝓕 : Filtration ℝ≥0 mΩ) (T : ℝ≥0) (M : Market Ω d) :
    Prop :=
  IsStronglyProgressive 𝓕 M.r ∧ IsStronglyProgressive 𝓕 M.b ∧ IsStronglyProgressive 𝓕 M.σ ∧
  (∃ η : ℝ, 0 ≤ η ∧ ∀ᵐ ω ∂P, ∀ t ≤ T, -η ≤ M.r t ω) ∧
  (∃ ε : ℝ, 0 < ε ∧ ∀ᵐ ω ∂P, ∀ t ≤ T, ∀ ξ : EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin d),
    ε * ‖ξ‖ ^ 2 ≤ inner ℝ ξ (Matrix.toEuclideanLin (M.σ t ω * (M.σ t ω).transpose) ξ)) ∧
  (∫⁻ ω, (∫⁻ s in Set.Icc (0 : ℝ) T, ENNReal.ofReal (M.r s.toNNReal ω)) ∂P < ∞) ∧
  (∫⁻ ω, (∫⁻ s in Set.Icc (0 : ℝ) T, ‖theta M s.toNNReal ω‖ₑ ^ 2) ∂P < ∞)

/-- (2.9): the discount process `γ₀(t) = exp(−∫₀ᵗ r(s) ds)`. -/
noncomputable def gamma0 (M : Market Ω d) (t : ℝ≥0) (ω : Ω) : ℝ :=
  Real.exp (-∫ s in Set.Icc (0 : ℝ) t, M.r s.toNNReal ω)

/-- (2.8): the exponential local martingale
`Z₀(t) = exp(−∫₀ᵗ θ*(s) dW(s) − ½∫₀ᵗ ‖θ(s)‖² ds)`, with the stochastic integral given by the
Itô-integral operator `I`. -/
noncomputable def Z0 (I : (ℝ≥0 → Ω → EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin d)) → ℝ≥0 → Ω → ℝ)
    (M : Market Ω d) (t : ℝ≥0) (ω : Ω) : ℝ :=
  Real.exp (-I (theta M) t ω - (1 / 2) * ∫ s in Set.Icc (0 : ℝ) t, ‖theta M s.toNNReal ω‖ ^ 2)

/-- (2.10): the state-price density `H₀(t) = γ₀(t) Z₀(t)`. -/
noncomputable def H0 (I : (ℝ≥0 → Ω → EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin d)) → ℝ≥0 → Ω → ℝ)
    (M : Market Ω d) (t : ℝ≥0) (ω : Ω) : ℝ :=
  gamma0 M t ω * Z0 I M t ω

/-- Definition 3.1 (i), p. 770: a portfolio process. -/
def IsPortfolio (𝓕 : Filtration ℝ≥0 mΩ) (P : Measure Ω) (T : ℝ≥0)
    (π : ℝ≥0 → Ω → EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin d)) : Prop :=
  IsLocallySquareIntegrable 𝓕 P T π

/-- Definition 3.1 (ii), p. 770: a nonnegative, progressively measurable process with
`∫₀ᵀ c(t) dt < ∞` a.s. -/
def IsConsumption (𝓕 : Filtration ℝ≥0 mΩ) (P : Measure Ω) (T : ℝ≥0) (c : ℝ≥0 → Ω → ℝ) :
    Prop :=
  IsStronglyProgressive 𝓕 c ∧ (∀ t ω, 0 ≤ c t ω) ∧
    ∀ᵐ ω ∂P, IntegrableOn (fun s : ℝ => c s.toNNReal ω) (Set.Icc (0 : ℝ) T)

/-- The wealth integrand `X(t) σ*(t) π(t)` of the `dW` part of (3.1). -/
noncomputable def wealthIntegrand (M : Market Ω d) (π : ℝ≥0 → Ω → EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin d))
    (X : ℝ≥0 → Ω → ℝ) (t : ℝ≥0) (ω : Ω) : EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin d) :=
  X t ω • Matrix.toEuclideanLin (M.σ t ω).transpose (π t ω)

/-- Definition 3.1 (iii) and (3.1)–(3.2), p. 770: `X` is the wealth process of `(π, c)` with
initial capital `x` in the market `𝓜`, i.e. a continuous adapted solution of
`dX = [rX − c] dt + X π*σ dW₀`, `dW₀ = dW + θ dt`, `X(0) = x`, written in integral form
`X(t) = x + ∫₀ᵗ (r X − c + X π*σθ) ds + ∫₀ᵗ X (σ*π)* dW`, both integrals existing. -/
def IsWealth (P : Measure Ω) (𝓕 : Filtration ℝ≥0 mΩ) (T : ℝ≥0)
    (I : (ℝ≥0 → Ω → EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin d)) → ℝ≥0 → Ω → ℝ) (M : Market Ω d) (x : ℝ)
    (π : ℝ≥0 → Ω → EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin d)) (c : ℝ≥0 → Ω → ℝ) (X : ℝ≥0 → Ω → ℝ) : Prop :=
  (∀ t, Measurable[𝓕 t] (X t)) ∧
  (∀ᵐ ω ∂P, ContinuousOn (fun t => X t ω) (Set.Iic T)) ∧
  IsLocallySquareIntegrable 𝓕 P T (wealthIntegrand M π X) ∧
  (∀ᵐ ω ∂P, IntegrableOn (fun s : ℝ =>
      M.r s.toNNReal ω * X s.toNNReal ω - c s.toNNReal ω +
        X s.toNNReal ω * inner ℝ (Matrix.toEuclideanLin (M.σ s.toNNReal ω).transpose
          (π s.toNNReal ω)) (theta M s.toNNReal ω)) (Set.Icc (0 : ℝ) T)) ∧
  ∀ t ≤ T, ∀ᵐ ω ∂P, X t ω = x +
    (∫ s in Set.Icc (0 : ℝ) t,
      (M.r s.toNNReal ω * X s.toNNReal ω - c s.toNNReal ω +
        X s.toNNReal ω * inner ℝ (Matrix.toEuclideanLin (M.σ s.toNNReal ω).transpose
          (π s.toNNReal ω)) (theta M s.toNNReal ω))) +
    I (wealthIntegrand M π X) t ω

/-- A portfolio/consumption pair together with a wealth process: `(π, c, X)`. -/
structure Triple (Ω : Type*) (d : ℕ) where
  π : ℝ≥0 → Ω → EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin d)
  c : ℝ≥0 → Ω → ℝ
  X : ℝ≥0 → Ω → ℝ

/-- Definition 3.2, p. 771: `𝒜₀(x)`, the admissible triples — `π` a portfolio, `c` a
consumption process, `X` its wealth process from capital `x`, and (3.3) `X(t) ≥ 0` for all
`0 ≤ t ≤ T`, almost surely. -/
def A0 (P : Measure Ω) (𝓕 : Filtration ℝ≥0 mΩ) (T : ℝ≥0)
    (I : (ℝ≥0 → Ω → EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin d)) → ℝ≥0 → Ω → ℝ) (M : Market Ω d) (x : ℝ) :
    Set (Triple Ω d) :=
  {τ | IsPortfolio 𝓕 P T τ.π ∧ IsConsumption 𝓕 P T τ.c ∧ IsWealth P 𝓕 T I M x τ.π τ.c τ.X ∧
    ∀ᵐ ω ∂P, ∀ t ≤ T, 0 ≤ τ.X t ω}

end CvitanicKaratzas92.Optimality


